  "rationale": "The current Pareto front is dominated by the `pipelined` family, with the best area at 39336 um^2 and accuracy up to 13.3 bits. The `pipelined_m` family failed to produce any feasible designs (0/40), likely due to throughput or latency constraints not being met in its specific configuration space. The `iterative` and `unrolled_k` families have not been explored yet. Given the strict throughput constraint (>= 32 MSPS) and the batch latency constraint (p99 <= 0.5 us), `iterative` (N+3 cycles/result) and `unrolled_k` (ceil(N/k)+3 cycles/result) are strong candidates for lower area if they can meet the throughput. For `iterative`, to get 32 MSPS, we need Fmax * (1/(N+3)) >= 32. If N=14, latency is 17 cycles. If Fmax is ~500 MHz, throughput is ~29 MSPS (fail). If Fmax is ~600 MHz, throughput is ~35 MSPS (pass). `unrolled_k` with k=2 or 3 reduces latency significantly. For example, N=14, k=3 -> 5+3=8 cycles. At 400 MHz, throughput is 50 MSPS. This suggests `unrolled_k` might offer a better area/throughput trade-off than `pipelined` (which has high FF count due to pipeline registers). I will explore `unrolled_k` and `iterative` to see if they can beat the 39336 um^2 area while maintaining feasibility. I will focus `unrolled_k` on moderate k (2-4) and N (12-16) to balance latency and area. I will also try `iterative` with higher N to ensure accuracy, relying on Fmax to meet throughput.",
